def les(**k): L.append(k)
les(id="role",t="Your two roles, decoded",tag="Start here",
an="Think of a conference-room camera bar as a restaurant. The diners (Zoom/Teams apps) only see the dining room. You work in the kitchen and the basement: the stoves (chips), the wiring (drivers), the delivery door (USB) and the rules for who may enter (security). The postings call this 'below the framework layer'.",
j=[("Firmware","Software that runs close to hardware, often stored in flash on the device."),("Framework layer","On Android, the Java/Kotlin system services apps talk to (CameraService, AudioFlinger, UsbService). 'Below' means HALs, native daemons, kernel, bootloader."),("BSP","Board Support Package: the vendor's bundle of bootloader, kernel, drivers and configs for a chip/board."),("HAL","Hardware Abstraction Layer: a fixed interface the OS calls, implemented by the device maker.")],
d="""<p>Both postings (Austin, Poly/HP) describe the same product family: <b>Android- and Linux-based video conferencing devices</b>. The USB role adds one hard requirement: <i>"In depth, hands-on, and extensive experience and knowledge on the full USB software stack is must-have."</i></p>
<h4>What 'full USB stack' means on a conferencing device</h4><ul>
<li><b>Device side</b> (the bar plugs into a laptop and shows up as webcam + mic + speaker + buttons): UDC controller driver (often Synopsys DWC3), Linux gadget framework, UVC/UAC2/HID functions, a userspace UVC streaming app, and Android's USB HAL that switches modes.</li>
<li><b>Host side</b> (the device hosts peripherals: extension mics, touch controllers, USB-C displays): xHCI, hub driver, class drivers, power budgeting.</li>
<li><b>Type-C/PD</b>: role swaps (host vs device), alternate modes (DisplayPort over USB-C), cable orientation.</li>
<li><b>Firmware update</b> over USB (DFU) and the MCU firmware inside accessories.</li></ul>
<h4>Shared responsibilities (both postings)</h4><ol>
<li>Design and develop firmware for Android/Linux devices.</li><li>Build platform capabilities below the framework.</li><li>Lead secure, scalable, high-performance embedded C/C++.</li><li>Define architecture, standards, best practices with cross-functional teams.</li><li>Troubleshoot complex system-level issues.</li><li>Planning, documentation, testing strategy.</li><li>Mentor junior engineers.</li><li>Agile/lean continuous improvement.</li></ol>
<p><b>Plus areas:</b> Networking/Wi-Fi, Security, USB, H.264/H.265/SVC, HDMI, Bluetooth, Android internals and Linux.</p>
<p>Your current work with NXP, Lontium HDMI bridges, Qualcomm and Ambarella vendor drivers maps directly onto 'HDMI', 'troubleshoot system-level issues' and 'BSP'. This course fills in the rest and focuses on the senior behaviors (lead, define standards, mentor) that the postings repeat.</p>""",
anim=("flow","The stack you own, top to bottom",["Apps (Teams/Zoom rooms)","Android framework services","HAL + native daemons (your code)","Linux kernel drivers (your code)","Bootloader / secure boot (your code)","SoC + bridges + USB PHY (vendor silicon)"],["Users only see this","Java system services call HALs","AIDL HALs, camera/audio/USB daemons","V4L2, ALSA, USB gadget, I2C bridges","ROM → SPL → U-Boot/ABL → kernel","Qualcomm/NXP/Ambarella, Lontium bridge"]),
q=[("Which layer does 'below the framework layer' NOT include?","The app layer (Teams/Zoom). Everything from HALs down is yours."),("What is the one must-have in the USB posting?","Deep, hands-on knowledge of the full USB software stack (device, host, class drivers, Android USB HAL, debugging).")],lab=None)

les(id="cpp",t="Embedded C/C++ craft that seniors are judged on",tag="C/C++",
an="Writing firmware is like cooking on a boat: the same recipe, but the floor moves (interrupts), the pantry is tiny (RAM) and if you spill (memory bug) nobody can come fix it once you sail (shipped devices).",
j=[("volatile","Tells the compiler a value can change outside normal code (hardware register). It does NOT make code thread-safe."),("Memory barrier","An instruction that stops the CPU/compiler from reordering memory accesses across it."),("ISR","Interrupt Service Routine: code the CPU jumps to when hardware signals."),("RAII","C++ idiom: a resource is released in the destructor, so cleanup happens automatically.")],
d="""<h4>Rules that prevent most field bugs</h4><ul>
<li><b>Register access</b>: use <code>readl()/writel()</code> in the kernel (they include the right barriers); in bare metal use <code>volatile</code> pointers plus explicit barriers (<code>__DMB()</code> on ARM) around DMA buffers.</li>
<li><b>ISR rules</b>: no blocking, no malloc, no printf; record and defer. Share data through lock-free single-producer/single-consumer rings (Lab 08) or with interrupts briefly disabled.</li>
<li><b>Atomics &amp; ordering</b>: use C11 <code>stdatomic</code> acquire/release: the producer writes data, then <i>releases</i> the index; the consumer <i>acquires</i> the index, then reads data.</li>
<li><b>Integer hygiene</b>: USB lengths are little-endian 16-bit (<code>wTotalLength</code>); always bounds-check descriptor lengths from the wire (a malformed descriptor is a classic security bug).</li>
<li><b>C++ in firmware</b>: RAII for fds/locks/mmaps in HAL daemons, <code>std::unique_ptr</code> with custom deleters, avoid exceptions/RTTI where the build forbids them, prefer <code>std::array</code>/<code>std::span</code>, <code>constexpr</code> tables for descriptors.</li>
<li><b>Tooling</b>: build with <code>-Wall -Wextra -Werror</code>; run ASan/UBSan/TSan on host-buildable parts; static analysis (clang-tidy, Coverity, cppcheck); in the kernel <code>sparse</code>, <code>checkpatch.pl</code>, KASAN, lockdep.</li></ul>
<h4>Senior signal</h4><p>Make host-testable seams: put protocol logic (descriptor parsing, state machines, CRC/image checks) in plain C files with no hardware calls, and unit-test them on the PC with sanitizers in CI. Every lab in this course follows that pattern.</p>""",
anim=("seq","ISR → task handoff through a lock-free ring",["Hardware","ISR","Ring","Task"],[(0,1,"IRQ fires"),(1,2,"write data[head]"),(1,2,"release head++"),(1,0,"ack IRQ, return fast"),(3,2,"acquire head"),(2,3,"read data[tail]"),(3,2,"release tail++")]),
q=[("Does volatile make a shared counter thread-safe?","No. It only stops the compiler caching it. You still need atomics or locks for read-modify-write."),("Why must the producer update head AFTER writing the slot (release)?","Otherwise the consumer could see the new head and read a slot that isn't written yet.")],lab="08-isr-ring-buffer")

les(id="drvmodel",t="The Linux driver model: bus, device, driver, probe",tag="Kernel",
an="A wedding seating plan: guests (devices) arrive with name tags (IDs). Ushers (buses) read the tag and walk them to the right table host (driver). When a match happens the host 'greets' them: that greeting is probe().",
j=[("Module","A .ko file loaded into the running kernel with insmod/modprobe."),("probe()","The driver function the kernel calls when a device matches it. It sets the device up."),("devm_*","Device-managed allocators: freed automatically when the device is unbound."),("sysfs","The /sys filesystem showing kernel objects as files."),("Char device","A device read/written as a byte stream via /dev, e.g. /dev/ttyACM0.")],
d="""<h4>Core objects</h4><ul><li><code>struct bus_type</code> (platform, i2c, spi, usb, pci) owns <code>match()</code>.</li><li><code>struct device</code>: a thing that exists (from DT, ACPI, USB enumeration).</li><li><code>struct device_driver</code>: the code that can run it. Match tables: <code>of_device_id</code> (DT compatible), <code>i2c_device_id</code>, <code>usb_device_id</code>.</li></ul>
<h4>Lifecycle</h4><p>register driver → bus match → <code>probe()</code> → device usable → <code>remove()</code> on unbind. If a dependency (regulator, clock, GPIO) isn't ready, return <code>-EPROBE_DEFER</code>; the kernel retries later. Use <code>dev_err_probe()</code> so deferrals don't spam logs. Check <code>/sys/kernel/debug/devices_deferred</code> when a device never appears: this is one of the most common bring-up bugs.</p>
<h4>User interfaces from a driver</h4><ul><li><b>Char/misc device</b> with <code>file_operations</code> (read/write/ioctl/poll/mmap).</li><li><b>sysfs attributes</b> (<code>DEVICE_ATTR_RO</code>, <code>sysfs_emit</code>) for one-value-per-file knobs.</li><li><b>Subsystem frameworks</b> instead of raw char devs whenever possible: V4L2 for video, ALSA for audio, input for buttons, USB gadget for device-mode USB. Reviewers (and upstream) will push back on custom ioctls when a framework exists.</li></ul>
<h4>Concurrency</h4><p>Any file op can run concurrently from multiple processes. Lab 01 protects its buffer with a mutex; <code>copy_to_user/copy_from_user</code> can sleep (page faults), so never call them while holding a spinlock.</p>""",
anim=("seq","Driver binding and probe",["Firmware/DT","Bus core","Driver","Userspace"],[(0,1,"device registered (compatible=poly,...)"),(2,1,"driver registered (of_match_table)"),(1,1,"match(): compatible strings equal"),(1,2,"probe(dev)"),(2,1,"needs GPIO not ready → -EPROBE_DEFER"),(1,2,"retry probe later → 0"),(2,3,"/dev node + sysfs attrs appear"),(3,2,"open/read/write/ioctl")]),
q=[("A driver's probe returns -EPROBE_DEFER. Is that an error?","Not necessarily: it means a dependency isn't ready yet; the core retries. It becomes a bug only if the dependency never arrives (check devices_deferred)."),("Why can't you call copy_from_user under a spinlock?","It may sleep on a page fault, and sleeping with a spinlock held can deadlock.")],lab="01-chardev-sysfs")

les(id="dt",t="Device Tree, platform drivers, I2C/SPI/GPIO, regmap",tag="Kernel",
an="The device tree is the house blueprint handed to the electrician (kernel): it says which room has which socket, wired to which breaker. The kernel can't 'see' an HDMI bridge on I2C; the blueprint has to say it is there.",
j=[("Device Tree (DT)","A text description (.dts) of hardware compiled to a binary (.dtb) that the bootloader passes to the kernel."),("compatible","The DT string the kernel uses to pick the driver, e.g. \"lontium,lt9611\"."),("Overlay","A DT patch applied on top of the base tree, e.g. for an add-on board."),("regmap","A kernel helper that wraps I2C/SPI register access with caching and locking."),("pinctrl","Configures which function a SoC pin has (GPIO, I2C, UART).")],
d="""<h4>Typical bridge node (Lontium-style)</h4><pre>&amp;i2c3 {
  hdmi-bridge@3b {
    compatible = "lontium,lt9611";
    reg = &lt;0x3b&gt;;                 /* I2C address */
    interrupts-extended = &lt;&amp;tlmm 50 IRQ_TYPE_EDGE_FALLING&gt;;
    reset-gpios = &lt;&amp;tlmm 52 GPIO_ACTIVE_HIGH&gt;;
    vdd-supply = &lt;&amp;vreg_1p8&gt;;
    ports { port@0 { reg=&lt;0&gt;; endpoint { remote-endpoint = &lt;&amp;dsi0_out&gt;; }; }; };
  };
};</pre>
<p>The driver then calls <code>devm_gpiod_get(dev,"reset",...)</code>, <code>devm_regulator_get(dev,"vdd")</code>, <code>devm_regmap_init_i2c()</code>, and links into the DRM bridge chain through the <code>ports</code> graph.</p>
<h4>Bus cheat sheet</h4><ul><li><b>I2C</b>: 2 wires, addressed, slow (100k-1MHz); bridges, PMICs, sensors, touch. Debug: <code>i2cdetect -y 3</code>, <code>i2cget</code>, check pull-ups and NACKs on a logic analyzer.</li><li><b>SPI</b>: 4 wires, faster, full-duplex; flash, some displays/codecs.</li><li><b>GPIO</b>: reset lines, enable pins, interrupts. Watch <code>GPIO_ACTIVE_LOW</code>: the gpiod API uses <i>logical</i> values, so active-low is handled for you.</li></ul>
<h4>Vendor-BSP reality</h4><p>Vendor trees (Qualcomm, NXP, Ambarella) carry many out-of-tree bindings. Senior practice: document every binding you add (YAML schema under Documentation/devicetree/bindings), run <code>make dtbs_check</code>, and keep board-specific values in DT instead of #ifdefs in C.</p>""",
anim=("flow","From blueprint to running driver",["board.dts written","dtc → board.dtb","Bootloader loads dtb + kernel","Kernel unflattens tree","platform/i2c device created","Driver matched by compatible","probe(): gpio/regulator/regmap"],["source text","binary blob","U-Boot: bootm/booti with fdt addr","of_platform_populate()","one struct device per node","of_match_table","resources come from DT properties"]),
q=[("Your bridge never probes and there's no error in dmesg. First two checks?","Is the node status=\"okay\" and does compatible exactly match the driver table? Then check /sys/kernel/debug/devices_deferred for a missing regulator/GPIO."),("Why use gpiod logical values instead of raw levels?","DT flags (ACTIVE_LOW) invert automatically, so the driver code stays board-independent.")],lab="02-platform-dt")

les(id="irq",t="Interrupts, threaded IRQs, workqueues, DMA, locking",tag="Kernel",
an="An interrupt is a doorbell. The top half is answering the door and signing for the package in seconds. The bottom half (threaded IRQ or workqueue) is unpacking it later in the kitchen, where you're allowed to take your time.",
j=[("Top half","The hard IRQ handler: runs with interrupts masked, must be very short, cannot sleep."),("Threaded IRQ","request_threaded_irq(): a kernel thread runs the slow part and may sleep (e.g. I2C reads)."),("Workqueue","A queue of deferred functions run in process context."),("DMA","Direct Memory Access: hardware copies data to RAM without the CPU."),("Spinlock","A lock that busy-waits; usable in interrupt context.")],
d="""<h4>Choosing a context</h4><table><tr><th>Need</th><th>Use</th></tr><tr><td>Device on I2C (bridge HPD, touch)</td><td><code>devm_request_threaded_irq(..., NULL, thread_fn, IRQF_ONESHOT, ...)</code>: I2C sleeps, so it must be threaded.</td></tr><tr><td>MMIO status register</td><td>Hard handler reads/acks, wakes thread or NAPI/tasklet.</td></tr><tr><td>Long jobs, retries, timers</td><td><code>schedule_work</code> / <code>delayed_work</code>.</td></tr></table>
<h4>Locking map</h4><ul><li>Data shared with a hard IRQ: <code>spin_lock_irqsave</code>.</li><li>Process-context only: <code>mutex</code>.</li><li>Single counters/flags: <code>atomic_t</code>, <code>READ_ONCE/WRITE_ONCE</code>.</li><li>Turn on <code>CONFIG_PROVE_LOCKING</code> (lockdep) in debug builds: it reports deadlock risks before they happen.</li></ul>
<h4>DMA essentials</h4><p>Use the DMA API, never physical addresses by hand: <code>dma_alloc_coherent</code> for small control rings, <code>dma_map_single/dma_map_sg</code> plus <code>dma_sync_*</code> for streaming buffers (cache maintenance on ARM!). For camera/video frames, buffers move between V4L2, the GPU and codec as <b>dma-buf</b> fds with zero copy. Missing cache sync shows up as 'green stripes' or stale lines in video.</p>
<h4>Teardown order</h4><p>On remove: disable the IRQ source, <code>free_irq</code>/devm, then <code>cancel_work_sync</code>, then free memory. Lab 03 shows <code>timer_delete_sync</code> then <code>cancel_work_sync</code>: wrong order can let the timer requeue work after you cancel it.</p>""",
anim=("seq","HDMI hot-plug interrupt on an I2C bridge",["Bridge chip","GIC/IRQ core","Hard handler","IRQ thread","DRM core"],[(0,1,"HPD pin falls"),(1,2,"mask line, call handler"),(2,1,"return IRQ_WAKE_THREAD"),(1,3,"wake thread"),(3,0,"I2C read status (sleeps)"),(3,0,"I2C write clear"),(3,4,"drm_bridge_hpd_notify()"),(3,1,"IRQ_HANDLED, unmask (ONESHOT)")]),
q=[("Why IRQF_ONESHOT for an I2C device's level IRQ?","The line stays masked until the thread finishes; otherwise a level IRQ would refire forever before the thread can clear it over I2C."),("Video shows stale stripes only on ARM board, not x86. Suspect?","Missing DMA cache maintenance (dma_sync_* or wrong buffer type): x86 DMA is coherent, many ARM paths aren't.")],lab="03-irq-workqueue")
